WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.268 --> 00:00:09.073
I've been writing about global health
and medical innovation for years,

00:00:09.073 --> 00:00:13.878
and when I tell my friends and family
how much progress there's been,

00:00:13.911 --> 00:00:16.547
the reaction is almost always the same.

00:00:16.547 --> 00:00:18.983
They had no idea
most of it was happening.

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.320
Take the most common cause
of death today, heart disease.

00:00:23.654 --> 00:00:27.792
People today have roughly a quarter
the chance of dying from heart disease

00:00:27.792 --> 00:00:30.795
as people did in the 1950s
at the same age.

00:00:31.162 --> 00:00:32.296
A quarter.

00:00:32.897 --> 00:00:37.201
Back then, there were no statins,
no cholesterol testing,

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:39.603
no implanted pacemakers,

00:00:39.637 --> 00:00:43.174
no anti-smoking campaigns,
no bans on trans fats,

00:00:43.174 --> 00:00:46.143
no bypass surgery, no CPR.

00:00:47.011 --> 00:00:48.446
But hardly anyone hears

00:00:48.479 --> 00:00:51.549
about the long-term impact
of those breakthroughs.

00:00:52.216 --> 00:00:54.385
When progress happens gradually,

00:00:54.418 --> 00:00:56.020
it's rarely considered news.

00:00:57.054 --> 00:00:58.656
And I think it's a problem.

00:00:58.689 --> 00:01:02.927
I think it’s part of why people
don’t know how important it’s all been --

00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:05.029
public health, vaccines
and all the rest --

00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:06.597
in improving people’s lives.

00:01:08.032 --> 00:01:09.700
But what I want to tell you about

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:12.269
is the thing that doesn't
make the news at all.

00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:14.371
Not the breakthroughs themselves,

00:01:14.405 --> 00:01:17.208
but everything that got
in the way of them.

00:01:18.476 --> 00:01:21.479
I used to have this impression
that breakthroughs were uncommon,

00:01:21.512 --> 00:01:22.880
sporadic,

00:01:22.913 --> 00:01:25.249
sometimes happening by pure chance,

00:01:25.282 --> 00:01:27.651
sometimes by sheer determination.

00:01:29.487 --> 00:01:31.388
The way I see it now,

00:01:31.422 --> 00:01:36.327
it's more like there's a continuous
stream of medical innovation every year.

00:01:37.595 --> 00:01:40.464
In just the last two years, for example,

00:01:40.498 --> 00:01:44.802
we've had a new antiviral against HIV,

00:01:44.835 --> 00:01:50.241
which protects against infections
with an efficacy of nearly 100 percent

00:01:50.274 --> 00:01:53.210
with just a single dose
given every six months.

00:01:54.111 --> 00:01:57.681
There are new drugs that reduce
cholesterol levels by 60 percent,

00:01:57.715 --> 00:01:59.316
beyond the effect of statins.

00:02:00.651 --> 00:02:04.822
There are new treatments that slow down
the progression of certain cancers,

00:02:04.855 --> 00:02:10.261
certain lung cancers, brain cancer
and multiple myeloma by half or more.

00:02:11.262 --> 00:02:13.564
And in just the last five years,

00:02:13.597 --> 00:02:17.635
we've had new vaccines against
four diseases for the very first time.

00:02:18.102 --> 00:02:19.236
COVID, of course,

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.772
but also the first malaria vaccine,

00:02:21.805 --> 00:02:26.076
the first chikungunya vaccine
and the first vaccine against RSV.

00:02:28.012 --> 00:02:32.082
The tools to develop new drugs
and vaccines have improved enormously.

00:02:32.983 --> 00:02:34.852
Like genome sequencing, for example,

00:02:34.885 --> 00:02:37.488
which has touched
almost every part of biology.

00:02:38.589 --> 00:02:42.326
Back when the Human Genome Project
was completed in 2003,

00:02:42.359 --> 00:02:47.064
it cost $50 million
to sequence one person’s genome,

00:02:47.097 --> 00:02:48.866
and it took half a year.

00:02:48.899 --> 00:02:52.269
Now it takes under four hours

00:02:52.303 --> 00:02:54.305
and it costs a few hundred dollars.

00:02:54.972 --> 00:02:58.943
There's also been a revolution
in the technology of microscopes.

00:02:58.943 --> 00:03:01.345
Over the past 200 years,

00:03:01.378 --> 00:03:05.883
their resolution has increased
over 10,000-fold.

00:03:06.283 --> 00:03:10.421
We can now see viruses
down to their individual atoms

00:03:10.454 --> 00:03:14.391
and design new drugs to target
them extremely precisely.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:17.728
This is RSV, respiratory syncytial virus.

00:03:18.062 --> 00:03:19.563
It's in the middle right there.

00:03:19.597 --> 00:03:22.633
And we can now see its atomic
structure on the right.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.835
Until the 1930s,

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:27.404
no one had ever seen a virus.

00:03:28.072 --> 00:03:33.244
So if our technology has advanced so much,

00:03:33.277 --> 00:03:36.380
why are so many diseases
still untreatable today?

00:03:38.682 --> 00:03:42.319
What I've learned is that technology
isn't always the barrier.

00:03:43.287 --> 00:03:47.491
Sometimes it’s about the funding,
the institutions and the incentives.

00:03:48.626 --> 00:03:51.161
We recently got a new
malaria vaccine, as I mentioned,

00:03:51.195 --> 00:03:54.465
and when I first heard
about it, I was amazed.

00:03:54.498 --> 00:03:56.033
I remember learning

00:03:56.066 --> 00:03:59.336
that malaria was a very
complicated disease, scientifically.

00:03:59.336 --> 00:04:02.806
It's caused by a parasite,
not a virus or bacterium.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.211
And that parasite changes shape
multiple times during its life cycle,

00:04:07.244 --> 00:04:10.748
which makes it really hard to know
what to target with a vaccine.

00:04:11.582 --> 00:04:14.418
So I wanted to write about it,
this amazing breakthrough.

00:04:15.586 --> 00:04:18.922
But one of the first things I learned
about this new malaria vaccine

00:04:18.922 --> 00:04:21.925
that was introduced just a few years ago,

00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:24.295
was that it was developed in the '90s.

00:04:24.928 --> 00:04:26.430
Decades ago.

00:04:26.964 --> 00:04:30.668
The researchers who developed it,
struggled to find funding to test it

00:04:30.701 --> 00:04:32.603
at every stage of the process.

00:04:33.103 --> 00:04:34.905
There was no commercial incentive.

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:38.042
It's not profitable to develop new drugs

00:04:38.075 --> 00:04:40.210
and vaccines against diseases

00:04:40.244 --> 00:04:42.813
that affect people in poverty,
in poorer countries,

00:04:43.213 --> 00:04:46.250
even if millions of children
might benefit.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:49.119
Even if there’s a huge
economic benefit as well.

00:04:50.187 --> 00:04:54.091
It took foreign aid and philanthropy
to fund the research to test it.

00:04:54.091 --> 00:04:57.728
And it literally took decades
to reach the children who needed it.

00:04:58.896 --> 00:05:01.031
When I learned about this,

00:05:01.031 --> 00:05:03.767
I felt like there was
nothing to celebrate.

00:05:03.801 --> 00:05:05.502
It seemed more like a failure.

00:05:06.603 --> 00:05:08.005
How could we let that happen?

00:05:08.038 --> 00:05:10.841
How could it take so long
to test a vaccine

00:05:10.874 --> 00:05:12.810
that had already been developed,

00:05:12.843 --> 00:05:16.814
while half a million children
were dying from malaria every year?

00:05:16.847 --> 00:05:18.282
Why wasn't that the story?

00:05:20.884 --> 00:05:23.020
Those children aren't coming back.

00:05:24.755 --> 00:05:29.193
But what I wanted to know was how we could
prevent that from happening again.

00:05:30.661 --> 00:05:33.831
Like, what if we could change
the economic incentives?

00:05:35.332 --> 00:05:38.702
Well, economists have come up
with an idea to do just that.

00:05:39.536 --> 00:05:42.439
It’s called an “advanced
market commitment.”

00:05:42.473 --> 00:05:46.844
It's where donors commit to buying
a vaccine at a certain price per dose,

00:05:46.844 --> 00:05:50.180
but only if it's developed
and proven safe and effective.

00:05:50.814 --> 00:05:54.518
That commitment gives companies
the confidence to invest in it

00:05:54.551 --> 00:05:56.220
in the first place,

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.188
and it can bring vaccines into existence

00:05:58.222 --> 00:06:00.758
that would otherwise never get made.

00:06:00.791 --> 00:06:01.959
And most importantly,

00:06:01.992 --> 00:06:04.495
it makes sure that they're
manufactured at scale

00:06:04.528 --> 00:06:06.563
and sold at an affordable price,

00:06:06.563 --> 00:06:08.699
so they reach children who need them.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.269
That idea was used over a decade ago

00:06:12.269 --> 00:06:15.005
to help develop new vaccines
against pneumococcal disease,

00:06:15.038 --> 00:06:18.142
which is a deadly
bacterial infection of the lungs.

00:06:19.243 --> 00:06:20.978
Vaccines already existed,

00:06:21.011 --> 00:06:25.516
but they didn't include the strains
that were common in Africa and South Asia.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:30.554
So in 2009, several countries
and philanthropists came together

00:06:30.587 --> 00:06:33.891
to fund an advanced market
commitment for new vaccines.

00:06:34.358 --> 00:06:35.559
And it actually worked.

00:06:35.559 --> 00:06:37.361
Several companies developed them,

00:06:37.394 --> 00:06:40.197
and they reached
children much faster than usual.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.001
It's estimated that those vaccines
have saved over 700,000

00:06:44.034 --> 00:06:45.936
children's lives since then.

00:06:45.969 --> 00:06:50.808
(Applause)

00:06:51.141 --> 00:06:56.346
This idea to fix market incentives
is one way to speed up the process.

00:06:57.147 --> 00:06:59.416
But it's not the only thing we can do.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:01.585
Take childhood leukemia, for example.

00:07:02.052 --> 00:07:05.923
It used to be very difficult
to test new treatments for the disease

00:07:05.956 --> 00:07:07.424
because leukemia is rare

00:07:07.458 --> 00:07:10.894
and individual hospitals struggled
to find enough patients

00:07:10.928 --> 00:07:12.362
to run clinical trials.

00:07:13.764 --> 00:07:15.999
So researchers built networks

00:07:16.033 --> 00:07:19.002
across the US and later Europe and Canada

00:07:19.002 --> 00:07:21.538
to help recruit patients
from all over those countries

00:07:21.572 --> 00:07:23.106
into larger clinical trials.

00:07:24.107 --> 00:07:26.510
That collaboration made it possible

00:07:26.543 --> 00:07:29.213
to test treatments
and learn what works faster.

00:07:30.581 --> 00:07:33.884
And it's why leukemia is no longer
the disease it used to be.

00:07:34.852 --> 00:07:36.687
Before the 1970s,

00:07:36.687 --> 00:07:40.958
only around 15 percent of children
with leukemia would survive

00:07:40.991 --> 00:07:43.327
even five years from a diagnosis.

00:07:43.694 --> 00:07:45.562
That's the bottom line on these graphs.

00:07:45.596 --> 00:07:47.130
Two types of leukemia.

00:07:47.464 --> 00:07:49.933
Now the top line --

00:07:49.967 --> 00:07:52.402
that figure is 85 percent.

00:07:53.003 --> 00:07:55.906
Most children in richer
countries today survive

00:07:55.906 --> 00:07:58.041
and are effectively cured of the disease.

00:07:58.976 --> 00:08:01.845
This graph is thousands of children

00:08:01.845 --> 00:08:04.414
who wouldn't have survived
without that collaboration,

00:08:04.414 --> 00:08:06.016
without that progress.

00:08:06.049 --> 00:08:08.252
I want to give you another example.

00:08:09.353 --> 00:08:11.588
Back when I was at university,

00:08:11.622 --> 00:08:15.826
we had a substitute lecturer one day
in my course on infectious diseases,

00:08:15.859 --> 00:08:19.062
and she told us that our original
lecturer was away

00:08:19.096 --> 00:08:21.532
because he had gone to West Africa

00:08:21.565 --> 00:08:24.368
to do research to develop
an Ebola vaccine.

00:08:24.935 --> 00:08:28.138
I remember sitting
in that lecture theater at the back,

00:08:28.138 --> 00:08:30.908
and I was thinking to myself,

00:08:30.941 --> 00:08:32.910
can you just do that?

00:08:32.943 --> 00:08:34.912
Can you just pack up and go?

00:08:35.546 --> 00:08:38.382
It hadn't even occurred to me,

00:08:38.382 --> 00:08:41.885
and it made me realize
that all of these breakthroughs

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:43.420
came from people like him,

00:08:43.453 --> 00:08:46.723
people who changed their lives
to make them happen,

00:08:46.723 --> 00:08:48.725
or people who thought differently,

00:08:48.759 --> 00:08:50.961
whether they were scientists, economists,

00:08:50.994 --> 00:08:52.629
operators or managers.

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:56.233
They created the incentives
and institutions

00:08:56.266 --> 00:08:59.369
so that generations
in the future, all of us,

00:08:59.369 --> 00:09:02.105
wouldn't have to face
the same problems that they did.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.742
And what's amazing is that we have
an Ebola vaccine now

00:09:05.776 --> 00:09:07.644
against the most common strain.

00:09:08.078 --> 00:09:11.448
It wasn't made by my lecturer,
but by researchers like him.

00:09:11.782 --> 00:09:14.785
And it was hard to develop and test it
for all the same reasons.

00:09:14.818 --> 00:09:16.887
There was little commercial incentive,

00:09:16.887 --> 00:09:18.722
and it was hard to run clinical trials

00:09:18.755 --> 00:09:21.792
because it was hard to predict
where Ebola outbreaks would arise

00:09:21.825 --> 00:09:23.727
and vaccinate people in advance.

00:09:24.361 --> 00:09:26.964
So scientists came up
with a different idea.

00:09:27.864 --> 00:09:31.201
They waited for individual
cases of Ebola to appear

00:09:31.201 --> 00:09:33.971
and then quickly vaccinated
all of the people around them

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:35.606
[and] tested the vaccine that way.

00:09:35.906 --> 00:09:38.241
That idea, called ring vaccination,

00:09:38.275 --> 00:09:41.478
helped find a vaccine
that we now know is very effective.

00:09:42.646 --> 00:09:47.050
Now these are just some of the ideas
that people have had so far.

00:09:48.285 --> 00:09:51.888
But what I've learned from them is
that breakthroughs don't just happen

00:09:51.922 --> 00:09:54.057
because we have the tools to make them.

00:09:54.458 --> 00:09:57.527
They depend on how innovation
happens in the real world.

00:09:58.762 --> 00:10:01.298
And there's something else
that I've learned from them

00:10:01.298 --> 00:10:03.133
that I find much harder to stomach.

00:10:04.001 --> 00:10:06.370
Many of them might not
have happened at all.

00:10:07.504 --> 00:10:11.708
So when I think back
to that stream of innovation,

00:10:11.742 --> 00:10:15.045
all of those breakthroughs and all
of that progress that we've seen,

00:10:15.078 --> 00:10:16.380
I do find it amazing.

00:10:16.413 --> 00:10:17.781
It's incredible.

00:10:18.248 --> 00:10:20.450
But it also really frustrates me.

00:10:21.551 --> 00:10:25.389
That's how much progress
we've made despite it being so hard.

00:10:26.223 --> 00:10:29.960
The good news is people have come up
with ideas to change that.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:33.063
Some of them really work,
like the ones I've told you about today,

00:10:33.096 --> 00:10:35.132
and they just haven't been used enough.

00:10:36.199 --> 00:10:38.669
But we also need people
to come up with new ideas

00:10:38.669 --> 00:10:40.604
and test them and scale them up too.

00:10:41.505 --> 00:10:43.940
I think it's more important
than ever that we do it,

00:10:43.974 --> 00:10:47.411
because of cuts to science,
global health and foreign aid,

00:10:47.411 --> 00:10:50.514
we have to find ways
to make our resources go further.

00:10:51.148 --> 00:10:52.949
Progress isn't inevitable.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:56.853
It takes people who decide
to persist despite that.

00:10:57.821 --> 00:10:59.523
And I think here it takes people

00:10:59.523 --> 00:11:01.892
who recognize something quite simple.

00:11:02.759 --> 00:11:05.228
That diseases are not a fact of life.

00:11:06.029 --> 00:11:08.398
They're problems that we can solve.

00:11:08.432 --> 00:11:09.700
Thank you.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:14.638
(Applause)

